1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:06,294
What is Git?

2
00:00:06,833 --> 00:00:14,143
Why version control exists, by first working without it, then doing the same work with Git and comparing.

3
00:00:15,900 --> 00:00:20,299
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

4
00:00:20,833 --> 00:00:24,163
First, the way many people start:

5
00:00:24,163 --> 00:00:26,382
copies with new names.

6
00:00:26,382 --> 00:00:27,593
Three files.

7
00:00:28,133 --> 00:00:29,468
Three files.

8
00:00:29,468 --> 00:00:31,803
Which one is current?

9
00:00:32,367 --> 00:00:35,452
One file, three recorded versions.

10
00:00:35,452 --> 00:00:38,446
Ask Git what the last change was:

11
00:00:38,446 --> 00:00:45,978
The commit knows its author, its date, its message, and exactly which line changed:

12
00:00:45,978 --> 00:00:48,246
-latte 3.20, +latte 3.30.

13
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:51,959
Let me try something.

14
00:00:51,959 --> 00:00:55,224
Delete the file, as if by accident:

15
00:00:55,767 --> 00:00:59,411
git status (lesson 08) shows D prices.txt:

16
00:00:59,411 --> 00:01:01,928
Git noticed the file is gone.

17
00:01:01,928 --> 00:01:06,441
In the no-Git folder, a deleted file is simply gone.

18
00:01:06,441 --> 00:01:12,081
Here, every committed version still exists inside the repository.

19
00:01:13,833 --> 00:01:15,633
Here is the fix.

20
00:01:17,367 --> 00:01:18,080
Got it.

21
00:01:18,080 --> 00:01:20,831
What do I need to remember?

22
00:01:21,367 --> 00:01:26,821
Git records snapshots (commits) with author, date and message.

23
00:01:27,367 --> 00:01:31,671
Any version can be compared with any other, and brought back.

24
00:01:32,233 --> 00:01:38,248
Copies named final-final cannot tell you what changed, who changed it, or why.

25
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:43,302
I keep hearing about Git vs GitHub.

26
00:01:43,302 --> 00:01:45,094
What is it, really?

27
00:01:45,633 --> 00:01:54,776
The difference between Git (a program on your computer) and GitHub (a website that hosts Git repositories and adds collaboration).

28
00:01:54,776 --> 00:02:00,543
We prove that Git works with no GitHub at all, then talk to GitHub with plain Git.

29
00:02:02,300 --> 00:02:06,700
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

30
00:02:07,233 --> 00:02:12,066
A complete repository with history, on this computer only.

31
00:02:12,066 --> 00:02:13,733
Is there any server?

32
00:02:14,267 --> 00:02:17,067
Three commits and zero remotes:

33
00:02:17,067 --> 00:02:20,139
no GitHub, no network, no account.

34
00:02:20,139 --> 00:02:27,456
Everything (history, branches, diffs) lives in the .git folder of this directory.

35
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:36,468
That push used Git alone, to a folder.

36
00:02:36,468 --> 00:02:51,845
GitHub does the same over the network, then adds a website around it.

37
00:02:53,600 --> 00:02:55,204
Let me try something.

38
00:02:55,204 --> 00:03:02,459
Push to a remote that does not exist (a typo in the path, a repository that was never created):

39
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:07,695
The error names the remote it tried and says it is not a repository.

40
00:03:07,695 --> 00:03:09,905
Check what the remote points to:

41
00:03:11,667 --> 00:03:15,102
Point the remote at a real repository (here:

42
00:03:15,102 --> 00:03:18,146
create it), or remove the wrong remote:

43
00:03:19,900 --> 00:03:20,613
Got it.

44
00:03:20,613 --> 00:03:23,364
What do I need to remember?

45
00:03:23,900 --> 00:03:30,884
Git is the version control program; it works on your computer, offline, with no account.

46
00:03:31,433 --> 00:03:36,188
GitHub hosts Git repositories and adds collaboration:

47
00:03:36,188 --> 00:03:40,673
pull requests, reviews, issues, Actions, releases.

48
00:03:41,233 --> 00:03:45,831
A remote is just "another copy of this repository somewhere":

49
00:03:45,831 --> 00:03:48,093
a folder, a server, or GitHub.

50
00:03:49,833 --> 00:03:52,288
How does installing Git work?

51
00:03:52,833 --> 00:03:56,333
How to install Git on Linux, macOS and Windows,

52
00:03:56,333 --> 00:04:01,248
check which Git you are running, and where it keeps its own files.

53
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:07,399
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

54
00:04:07,933 --> 00:04:11,923
Which git runs, and where are its helper programs?

55
00:04:12,467 --> 00:04:15,866
Which git runs, and where are its helper programs?

56
00:04:15,866 --> 00:04:21,576
Each git <command> is a built-in or a program called git-<command> in the exec path.

57
00:04:22,133 --> 00:04:28,275
Each git <command> is a built-in or a program called git-<command> in the exec path.

58
00:04:28,275 --> 00:04:30,688
That is also how extensions work:

59
00:04:30,688 --> 00:04:36,098
install a program named git-lfs and git lfs becomes a command (lesson 88).

60
00:04:37,833 --> 00:04:39,567
Let me try something.

61
00:04:39,567 --> 00:04:44,273
Call a Git command that does not exist, the classic typo:

62
00:04:44,833 --> 00:04:49,658
Git says it is not a command and usually suggests the closest real one.

63
00:04:49,658 --> 00:04:57,404
The same message appears for commands that come from an extension you have not installed (git lfs without Git LFS,

64
00:04:57,404 --> 00:04:59,918
git filter-repo without filter-repo).

65
00:05:01,667 --> 00:05:03,135
Use the right name.

66
00:05:03,135 --> 00:05:09,316
To catch typos automatically, Git can run the closest match after a short delay:

67
00:05:11,067 --> 00:05:11,780
Got it.

68
00:05:11,780 --> 00:05:14,531
What do I need to remember?

69
00:05:15,067 --> 00:05:20,881
Install Git with your package manager or the official installer; check with git --version.

70
00:05:21,433 --> 00:05:25,455
git <command> maps to built-ins and git-<command> programs:

71
00:05:25,455 --> 00:05:27,568
that is how extensions plug in.

72
00:05:28,133 --> 00:05:34,043
Typos produce "is not a git command"; -h and git help are always available offline.

73
00:05:35,800 --> 00:05:38,809
Why would I need first Git configuration?

74
00:05:39,367 --> 00:05:44,949
How to tell Git who you are, and how Git combines settings from three levels:

75
00:05:44,949 --> 00:05:46,761
system, global and local.

76
00:05:48,500 --> 00:05:52,899
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

77
00:05:53,433 --> 00:05:57,828
Set your identity once, globally (use your own name and e-mail):

78
00:05:57,828 --> 00:06:06,343
init.defaultBranch main makes every new repository start with a branch called main (older Git versions defaulted to master).

79
00:06:06,343 --> 00:06:08,678
Where does each setting come from?

80
00:06:09,233 --> 00:06:17,834
init.defaultBranch main makes every new repository start with a branch called main (older Git versions defaulted to master).

81
00:06:17,834 --> 00:06:20,193
Where does each setting come from?

82
00:06:20,733 --> 00:06:23,701
(The local lines come from the lab script:

83
00:06:23,701 --> 00:06:31,757
it gave this repository an identity because none existed globally yet.) Now a project that needs another identity,

84
00:06:31,757 --> 00:06:35,291
for example your work e-mail in a work repository.

85
00:06:35,291 --> 00:06:39,955
A local setting overrides the global one, in this repository only:

86
00:06:39,955 --> 00:06:43,488
The global value still exists; the local one wins.

87
00:06:45,233 --> 00:06:47,092
Let me try something.

88
00:06:47,092 --> 00:06:52,493
A fresh machine (or a new CI runner) with no identity at all.

89
00:06:53,033 --> 00:06:57,417
Please tell me who you are / Author identity unknown:

90
00:06:57,417 --> 00:07:00,725
no user.name or user.email at any level.

91
00:07:00,725 --> 00:07:02,628
Check what Git can see:

92
00:07:04,367 --> 00:07:11,072
Configure the identity (globally on your own machine; in CI, set it in the job).

93
00:07:11,072 --> 00:07:12,916
Then the commit works:

94
00:07:14,667 --> 00:07:15,380
Got it.

95
00:07:15,380 --> 00:07:18,131
What do I need to remember?

96
00:07:18,667 --> 00:07:24,886
user.name and user.email go into every commit; set them before your first commit.

97
00:07:25,433 --> 00:07:26,436
Three levels:

98
00:07:26,436 --> 00:07:28,288
system < global < local.

99
00:07:28,288 --> 00:07:33,458
The most specific wins; --show-origin --show-scope tells you which.

100
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:38,909
init.defaultBranch main names the first branch of new repositories.
